From 1e730819794f539c9bc2aa868ead0a655ce63d86 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 15:35:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 36/43] PCI: apple-h9p: Opt-in system sleep (s2idle)

Without PM callbacks, a system suspend runs the endpoints' callbacks
and genpd then powers the PCIe domains off under live links; a BAR
access afterwards can stall the CPU. Add pcie_apple_h9p.suspend:

- 0 (default): .prepare refuses system sleep (-EBUSY); nothing else
  changes.
- 1: every port stays powered and linked. NVMe shuts down (CC.SHN) and
  comes back through its reset path; brcmfmac does its D3 handshake.
  On resume_noirq, before any endpoint is touched, each port's PERST#
  and link status are checked (a lost link is retrained) and the NVMMU
  registers are compared with what was programmed (re-run if lost).
- 2: as 1, but ports with an endpoint power GPIO (Wi-Fi) take the iOS
  AppleS800xPCIePort::disableGated sequence in suspend_noirq:
  PME_Turn_Off and its ack, L2, PERST#, refclk/power/enable off. The
  chip itself stays powered (REG_ON is on the PMU), so resume retrains
  the link and brcmfmac reloads the firmware.
- 3: as 2 for every port, NVMe included (experimental).

Endpoints and root ports are held in D0 (PCI_DEV_FLAGS_NO_D3 between
prepare and complete): D3hot would take the links to L1, which these
ports don't do (aspm-no-l1). A port that does not come back is cut off:
PERST#, port off, config access below it refused, and every device
under it marked disconnected, so the PCI core and the drivers leave the
dead link alone. The genpd virtual devices are syscore so that the
domains stay on; the driver takes the links down itself.

Untested on hardware.
